      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, the Meridian & Bigbee Railroad ran through Pennington, linking the town to regional trade routes and spurring local commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pennington_alabama",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Covering just over five square miles, Pennington’s borders are shaped by the Tombigbee River, which flows through the town’s modest water area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/pennington_alabama",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pennington experiences a humid subtropical climate, bringing long, hot, humid summers and mild, short winters with occasional cold snaps.",
